course: dictionary
lesson: 16161
title: An old word: wive
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Wive is an old word that means to marry, or to take a wife. People today say marry instead, so you will rarely hear wive in everyday talk.
explain: «Long ago, the young knight vowed to wive the fair maiden.» The clue is wife. Wive is linked to wife, so it means to marry her and make her his wife.
explain: You will mostly meet wive in very old writing, such as Shakespeare's plays and old ballads. You will much more often see «wives», the plural of wife.
explain: Check the meaning by swapping in marry: «Long ago, the young knight vowed to marry the fair maiden.» It still makes sense, so marry is a good match for wive.
fact: Wive is a verb, which means a doing word.
fact: Wive means to marry a woman, or to take a wife.
fact: Wive is archaic, which means it is old and hardly used now.
fact: «Wives» is the plural of «wife», meaning more than one wife.
fact: In everyday English, say marry instead of wive.
exercise: «The old king said he would wive the fair princess.» What does wive mean here? | *To marry | To leave the castle | To cook a feast | To sing a loud song
exercise: Which word is the plural of wife? | wife's | *wives | wiving | wived
exercise: Which modern word would you use instead of wive? | Travel | Hide | *Marry | Shout
test:
«In the old ballad, the knight longed to wive his true love.» What does wive mean? | *To marry | To travel far away | To forget a promise | To wave goodbye
Which word would you say today in place of wive? | Sing | Wave | *Marry | Drop
exam:
Which word is closest in meaning to wive? | Shake | Hide | *Marry | Shout
